 [17592186495073 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95228" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495073 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495073 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $18?$\n\nI. ${R}_{17}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $17$\n\nII. ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 3}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 3$ matrices over $R$\n\nIII. ${R}^{17}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495073 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "300def0f-69c7-5821-80a9-ead2c4071d95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495073 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}_{17}[t])=17+1=18.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6\\times 3}(R))=6\\cdot 3=18.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}^{17})=17\\ne 18.\\,\\times \n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495081 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186495005 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495081 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106882" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495081 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495081 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 5}(R),$ the vector space of all $6\\times 5$ matrices over $R?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495081 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4868b354-15b9-5c4c-bfd0-122202f40c50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495081 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{6\\times 5}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{6\\times 5}(R))=6\\cdot 5=30.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186495107 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186494973 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495107 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95225" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495107 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495107 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces has a dimension of $12?$\n\nI. ${R}^{12}$\n\nII. ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 4}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 4$ matrices over $R$\n\nIII. ${R}_{11}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $11$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495107 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "872bbc86-b2d9-5fc0-a340-666aa227cedf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495107 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of the vector spaces in turn.\n\nI. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}^{n})=n.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}^{12})=12.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 4}(R))=3\\cdot 4=12.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nIII. Recall that $\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.$ Therefore, \n\n  $$\n  \\text{dim}({R}_{11}[t])=11+1=12.\\,✓\n  $$\n\nHence, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495209 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186495231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495209 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6ac3edfc-26f8-589b-8f2c-b805c5ecd692" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495209 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186495107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186495217 #edb/ref