77 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562384 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186562153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562384 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fee18a9e-3a2d-5f93-9c78-89a367b51148" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562414 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562414 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95215" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562414 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562414 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R),$ the vector space of all $3\\times 9$ matrices over $R?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562414 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2ca0b310-c54f-553d-a3d3-7e3881bc581e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562414 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{3\\times 9}(R))=3\\cdot 9=27.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562418 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562418 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106869" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562418 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562418 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of ${R}_{100}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $100?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562418 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30dcac50-046c-59c3-a80d-0b2281d525fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562418 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{100}[t]$ is \n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{100}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{100}[t])=100+1=101.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562419 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562242 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562419 #edb/ref 1069 "q-36479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562419 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562419 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of ${R}_{8}[t]$, the vector space of all polynomials in the variable $t$ of degree at most $8?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562419 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30efcbe6-dda5-5a19-8bde-8531afb9c0c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562419 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of ${R}_{n}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{n}\\}.\n$$\n\nSo, the standard basis of ${R}_{8}[t]$ is\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,\\,{t}^{8}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have that\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{8}[t])=8+1=9.\n$$\n\nIn general,\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}_{n}[t])=n+1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562357 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562420 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106906" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562420 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following vector spaces are finite-dimensional?\n\nI. ${\\text{M}}_{500}(R),$ the vector space of all $500\\times 500$ matrices over $R$\n\nII. $C[0,1],$ the vector space of all continuous functions $f:[0,1]\\to R$\n\nIII. ${R}^{1500}$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "317a01e3-d69d-5965-940a-bd52e056d6a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562420 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our vector spaces.\n\n- ${\\text{M}}_{500}(R)$ and ${R}^{1500}$  are finite-dimensional. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{500}(R))={500}^{2}=250\\,000,\n$$\n\n \nand\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({R}^{1500})=1\\,500\n$$\n\nare both finite numbers.\n\n- $C[0,1]$ is *not* finite-dimensional. To understand why, we first note that the following set of (polynomial) functions is linearly independent:\n\n$$\n\\{1,\\,t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,…,{t}^{n}\\},\\,n\\in N.\n$$\n\nIndeed, we have that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=0}^{n}{x}_{i}{t}^{i}=0\n$$\n\nfor all $t\\in [0,1]$ if and only if ${x}_{i}=0$ for all $i=0,1,2,…n.$ Therefore,  for any  $n\\in N,$  there exists a linearly independent subset of $C[0,1]$ of size $n,$ which imples that $C[0,1]$ cannot have finite dimension.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562429 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562429 #edb/ref 1069 "q-106884" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562429 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562429 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R),$ the vector space of all $4\\times 9$ matrices over $R?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562429 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "43503f70-a039-563c-bedc-d1b2782aedca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562429 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R)$ contains a total of $n\\cdot m$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{3\\times 2}(R)$ contains $3\\cdot 2=6$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{4\\times 9}(R))=4\\cdot 9=36.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n\\times m}(R))=n\\cdot m.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562451 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186562330 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562451 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562451 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562451 #edb/ref 1071 "What is the dimension of ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ the vector space of all $7\\times 7$ matrices over $R?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562451 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7efa12c8-5625-54e7-8464-2de1d85a3ba0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186562451 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{n}(R)$ contains a total of ${n}^{2}$ matrices, each with one nonzero entry that is equal to $1.$ \n\nFor example, the standard basis of ${\\text{M}}_{2}(R)$ contains ${2}^{2}=4$ elements, and is given by\n\n$$\n\\{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${\\text{M}}_{7}(R),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{7}(R))={7}^{2}=49.\n$$\n\nIn general, \n\n$$\n\\text{dim}({\\text{M}}_{n}(R))={n}^{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
